</rich_text>
    <rich_text scale="h2">1. DATABASE OVERVIEW</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Database Management System:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> MySQL
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Database Management Tool:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> MySQL Workbench
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Application:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> SchemaX Student Information System
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Programming Language:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> C# (.NET 10 Windows Forms)
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Assigned Developer:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Reynalyn Samar
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Database Name:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> schemax_sis
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Local Database Setup:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> VERIFIED
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Application Database Integration:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> VERIFIED FOR TESTED FEATURES
The schemax_sis database stores student records, academic information, attendance data, and registered user accounts.
The initial database structure was created using the project's SQL setup script. Additional local schema updates were required during final integration testing to support the latest login and dashboard features.


</rich_text>
    <rich_text scale="h2">2. DATABASE STRUCTURE</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
The following five application tables were verified in MySQL Workbench on October 22, 2026.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">1. students</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Stores student personal information, academic details, and account ownership.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">2. subjects</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Stores subject information, including subject identifiers, titles, and units.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">3. student_subjects</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Associates students with subjects and stores related academic information, including grades.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">4. attendance</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Stores student attendance records.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">5. users</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Stores registered application user accounts and password hashes.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Storage Engine:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> InnoDB
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Character Set:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> utf8mb4
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Collation:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> utf8mb4_unicode_ci
The original database schema includes primary keys, unique constraints, foreign keys, and a year-level check constraint.
The exact constraints of the newly created users table should be included in the final SQL setup script.


</rich_text>
    <rich_text scale="h2">3. STUDENT RECORD INFORMATION</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
The students table contains the following columns:
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">student_id:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Student identifier and primary key
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">first_name:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Student's first name
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">middle_name:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Optional middle name
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">surname:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Student's surname
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">gender:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Student's gender
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">year_level:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Current year level
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">program:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Academic program
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">bloc:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Student's block
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">birthday:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Date of birth
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">bu_email:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> University email address
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">contact_no:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Contact number
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">photo_path:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Optional photo path
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">created_at:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Record creation timestamp
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">user_id:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Identifier of the registered user associated with the student record

</rich_text>
    <rich_text scale="h3">Student Ownership Integration</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
The latest application uses user_id to associate student records with a logged-in account.
During October 22 testing, the existing students table did not contain this column.
The column was added locally, and the two existing student records were assigned to the registered test account.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Verification Result:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> PASS
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">Records Preserved:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> 2


</rich_text>
    <rich_text scale="h2">4. USER ACCOUNT INFORMATION</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
The users table supports application registration and authentication.
The following columns were implemented in the local testing environment:
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">user_id:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Integer primary key with automatic numbering
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">first_name:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Registered user's first name
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">surname:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Registered user's surname
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">bu_email:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Registered user's university email address
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">username:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Unique account username
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">password_hash:</rich_text>
    <rich_text> Stored password hash

</rich_text>
    <rich_text scale="h3">User Account Operations</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">UserRepository.UsernameExists()</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Checks whether a username already exists.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">UserRepository.EmailExists()</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Checks whether a university email is already registered.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">UserRepository.Create()</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Creates a new user account.
</rich_text>
    <rich_text weight="heavy">UserRepository.Authenticate()</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
Checks login credentials and retrieves the authenticated user.

</rich_text>
    <rich_text scale="h3">Security Implementation</rich_text>
    <rich_text>
The application uses a PasswordHasher helper to hash passwords before storing them.
The repository also uses parameterized SQL queries to handle user-provided values.
The users table enforces unique usernames and university email addresses.
</rich_text>
